cess","api_error_status":null,"result":"**ETF liquidation ≠ losing everything.** When a US '40 Act ETF closes, the fund sells its holdings, pays expenses, and distributes the remaining net assets pro rata to shareholders, usually in cash. Your loss is limited to any decline in NAV. A total loss would require the underlying assets themselves to become worthless, which is rare for diversified funds.\n\n**Amount:** You receive your share of NAV at liquidation, after costs. That figure may be above or below your purchase price.\n\n**Timing:** The sponsor announces a last trading day and a later liquidation or distribution date. The schedule varies by fund, so check the fund's notice or prospectus supplement. You can also sell on-exchange before delisting, which may involve wider spreads.\n\n**Taxes (taxable accounts):** Liquidation generally counts as a sale. You realize a capital gain or loss versus your cost basis, short- or long-term depending on holding period. A final distribution may include separately taxed capital gains or income.","ttft_ms":1158,"type":"result","duration_ms":4638,"uuid":"29875cc1-7ea4-41ef-9e0d-03219d16ec80","ttft_stream_ms":1114,"time_to_request_ms":71,"first_content_frame_ms":1114,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0,"credential_expires_at":"2026-11-28T06:36:39.484Z"}
